Apple has blocked compromised security certificates on its devices that could help attackers impersonate websites, <a href="https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/mozilla-google-apple-block-compromised-emudhra-ca-certificates/"><em>BleepingComputer</em> reports</a>.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/03/apple-lock-security-bug-vulnerability-fix-privacy.jpg" alt="" width="2016" height="1134" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1038581" /><br/>
The certificates were issued by certificate authority eMudhra. According to <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=2083449">Mozilla's investigation</a>, their private keys were all numbers below 256, making them almost instantly recoverable. Attackers could use the affected website certificate keys to create fraudulent certificates that vulnerable software would trust.<br/>
<br/>
Apple introduced the blocks on October 19, according to an <a href="https://emudhra.we-have-a-problem.com/">advisory</a> from Mozilla cryptography engineer Dennis Jackson. These protections stop software using Apple's system checks from trusting the compromised certificates.<br/>
<br/>
Jackson says Safari and other major browsers had additional certificate checks that would have prevented attacks through this route. Whether attackers exploited other software remains unknown.<br/>
<br/>
